[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Gatsby: Static Site Generator with React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gatsby is a free and open-source static site generator based on React. It enables developers to build blazing-fast websites and apps by leveraging modern web technologies like React, GraphQL, and Webpack. This tutorial will guide you through the fundamentals of Gatsby, from installation to deployment."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager) or yarn installed on your machine."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of React.js concepts."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Familiarity with the command line."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation and Setup"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, you need to install the Gatsby CLI (Command Line Interface) globally. Open your terminal and run the following command:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"npm install -g gatsby-cli"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the Gatsby CLI is installed, you can create a new Gatsby project using the `gatsby new` command. Replace `my-gatsby-site` with your desired project name:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"gatsby new my-gatsby-site"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command will create a new directory named `my-gatsby-site` with a basic Gatsby project structure. Navigate into the project directory:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"cd my-gatsby-site"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, start the development server:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"gatsby develop"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This will start the Gatsby development server, and you can view your site in your browser at `http://localhost:8000`."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Gatsby Project Structure"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the project structure is crucial for building Gatsby sites:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`src/pages`: This directory contains React components that automatically become pages on your site. Each file in this directory will be a route (e.g., `src/pages/index.js` becomes `/`, and `src/pages/about.js` becomes `/about`)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`src/components`: This directory is for reusable React components that are used across your site."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`gatsby-config.js`: This file contains the configuration for your Gatsby site, including site metadata, plugins, and other settings."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`gatsby-node.js`: This file allows you to implement Gatsby's Node APIs, which are used to create pages dynamically, modify the GraphQL schema, and more."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`gatsby-browser.js`: This file lets you implement Gatsby's browser APIs, allowing you to customize the browser environment."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`gatsby-ssr.js`: This file lets you implement Gatsby's server-side rendering APIs, allowing you to customize the HTML output."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Pages"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gatsby automatically creates pages from React components in the `src/pages` directory. Let's create a simple 'About' page."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new file named `about.js` in the `src/pages` directory with the following content:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"import React from "react"

const AboutPage = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>About Us</h1>
      <p>This is the about page.</p>
    </div>
  )
}

export default AboutPage"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, navigate to `http://localhost:8000/about` in your browser. You should see the 'About Us' page."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Components"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new component in the `src/components` directory. For example, create a file named `MyComponent.js`:"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

const MyComponent = ({ text }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>{text}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, import and use this component in your `index.js` page:"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import MyComponent from '../components/MyComponent';

const IndexPage = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello, Gatsby!</h1>
      <MyComponent text="This is a custom component." />
    </div>
  );
};

export default IndexPage;"},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Gatsby Plugins"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gatsby plugins are packages that add functionality to your Gatsby site. There are plugins for various purposes, such as image optimization, data sourcing, and SEO."},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"To install a plugin, use npm or yarn. For example, to install `gatsby-plugin-image` (for image optimization), run:"},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"npm install gatsby-plugin-image gatsby-plugin-sharp gatsby-source-filesystem gatsby-transformer-sharp"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, add the plugin to your `gatsby-config.js` file:"},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  plugins: [
    `gatsby-plugin-image`,
    `gatsby-plugin-sharp`,
    `gatsby-transformer-sharp`,
    {
      resolve: `gatsby-source-filesystem`,
      options: {
        name: `images`,
        path: `${__dirname}/src/images`,
      },
    },
  ],
}"},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"GraphQL and Data Fetching"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gatsby uses GraphQL to fetch data. You can query data from various sources, such as Markdown files, APIs, and databases."},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install `gatsby-source-filesystem` and `gatsby-transformer-remark` to read Markdown files:"},{"order":44,"type":"code","text":"npm install gatsby-source-filesystem gatsby-transformer-remark"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"Add them to `gatsby-config.js`:"},{"order":46,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  plugins: [
    {
      resolve: `gatsby-source-filesystem`,
      options: {
        name: `markdown-pages`,
        path: `${__dirname}/src/markdown-pages`,
      },
    },
    `gatsby-transformer-remark`,
  ],
}"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a directory `src/markdown-pages` and add a Markdown file, e.g., `src/markdown-pages/my-page.md`:"},{"order":48,"type":"code","text":"---
title: "My Markdown Page"
---

# Hello from Markdown!

This is a sample Markdown page."},{"order":49,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, you can query this data in your page components using the `useStaticQuery` hook:"},{"order":50,"type":"code","text":"import React from "react"
import { graphql, useStaticQuery } from "gatsby"

const MyPage = () => {
  const data = useStaticQuery(graphql`
    query {
      markdownRemark(frontmatter: { title: { eq: "My Markdown Page" } }) {
        frontmatter {
          title
        }
        html
      }
    }
  `)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{data.markdownRemark.frontmatter.title}</h1>
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: data.markdownRemark.html }} />
    </div>
  )
}

export default MyPage"},{"order":51,"type":"subheading","text":"Building and Deploying"},{"order":52,"type":"paragraph","text":"To build your Gatsby site for production, run:"},{"order":53,"type":"code","text":"gatsby build"},{"order":54,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command creates an optimized static site in the `public` directory. You can then deploy the contents of this directory to various hosting providers, such as Netlify, Vercel, or AWS S3."},{"order":55,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, deploying to Netlify is as simple as dragging and dropping the `public` folder to the Netlify website."},{"order":56,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":57,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gatsby is a powerful static site generator that simplifies building fast and modern websites with React. This tutorial covered the basics of installation, project structure, page creation, component usage, plugins, data fetching with GraphQL, and deployment. Explore the official Gatsby documentation for more advanced features and customization options."}]
